diff --git a/docs/victoriametrics/README.md b/docs/victoriametrics/README.md
index 6d503e8..d94015b 100644
--- a/docs/victoriametrics/README.md
+++ b/docs/victoriametrics/README.md
@@ -2217,6 +2217,30 @@ The exceeded limits can be [monitored](#monitoring) with the following metrics:
 
 These limits are approximate, so VictoriaMetrics can underflow/overflow the limit by a small percentage (usually less than 1%).
 
+### Identifying metric names hitting the series limit
+
+When `-storage.maxHourlySeries` or `-storage.maxDailySeries` is exceeded, it is often useful to know *which* metric names
+are responsible for the dropped samples. VictoriaMetrics tracks this automatically: whenever a sample is dropped because
+of one of these limits, a per-metric-name counter of dropped samples is incremented.
+
+This tracking is controlled by the `-storage.trackDroppedSeriesByName` command-line flag, which is enabled by default.
+It can be disabled if it isn't needed, in which case it doesn't add any measurable overhead to data ingestion.
+
+The tracked data is stored in memory and its size is bounded, so it cannot grow without bound even if the offending
+installation has millions of distinct metric names. The memory limit defaults to 0.1% of the memory available to
+VictoriaMetrics and can be overridden with the `-storage.cacheSizeDroppedSeriesByName` command-line flag. Once the
+limit is reached, samples dropped for metric names that are already tracked keep incrementing their counters, while
+samples dropped for previously unseen metric names are no longer recorded until the tracker is reset.
+
+The collected data can be queried via the `/internal/series_limit_exceeded` HTTP endpoint on `vmsingle` or `vmstorage`,
+which returns up to `limit` (10 by default) metric names with the highest number of dropped samples:
+
+```sh
+curl http://localhost:8428/internal/series_limit_exceeded?limit=10
+```
+
+The tracked data can be reset via the `/internal/series_limit_exceeded/reset` HTTP endpoint.
+
 See also more advanced [cardinality limiter in vmagent](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/vmagent/#cardinality-limiter)
 and [cardinality explorer docs](#cardinality-explorer).
 
